# Scene Size-up and Safety (NREMT EMR test plan, 2025: 19% to 23%).
# Job tasks: plan before arrival from dispatch information; protect self, other responders,
# patient and public from hazards; don PPE for known hazards; find every patient; triage;
# request resources. EMR scope (National EMS Scope of Practice Model 2019 with change notices):
# emergency moves for endangered patients and auto-injector antidotes for chemical exposures are
# EMR skills. Triage follows SALT as printed in the NASEMSO National Model EMS Clinical
# Guidelines v3.0 (Mass Casualty Triage). All items original.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 1, "Scene safety", "You are first on scene at a car that struck a utility pole. A wire lies across the hood and the driver is awake inside. What should you do?",
      ["Stay back and tell him to stay in the car", "Pull him out through the far door, away from the wire", "Lift the wire off the hood with a dry branch", "Reach in through the window to hold his head still"],
      "Treat every downed wire as live. A person in the car is usually safer inside until the power company makes the line safe, and you stay well clear. Wood can carry current, and touching the car puts you in the circuit."),
    S(1, 2, "Dispatch information", "Dispatch reports a fall from a ladder and adds that a caller heard shouting and a gunshot before the fall. What is the safest plan?",
      ["Stage nearby until police secure the scene", "Enter quickly, since a patient is down and hurt", "Park at the door with lights off and go straight in", "Ask a bystander to check the patient"],
      "A report of a gunshot means the scene may not be safe. You stage out of sight and wait for law enforcement to say it is secure. Sending a bystander in only moves the danger to someone else."),
    S(1, 2, "Standard precautions", "Which PPE should you put on before caring for a patient coughing up blood after an assault?",
      ["Gloves, eyewear and a mask", "Gloves alone", "A gown alone", "No PPE until blood is on you"],
      "Blood that can be coughed or sprayed calls for protection of the eyes, nose and mouth as well as the hands. PPE is chosen before contact, from what you expect, not after an exposure."),
    S(1, 2, "Airborne precautions", "A man with a long cough, night sweats and weight loss needs care. If he can tolerate it, what goes on him?",
      ["A surgical mask", "A nonrebreather mask at 2 L/min", "A fit-tested N95 respirator", "Nothing, to avoid upsetting him"],
      "Signs of possible tuberculosis call for source control: a surgical mask on the patient limits the droplets he breathes out, while you wear a fit-tested N95. An N95 is for the rescuer, and a nonrebreather needs a high flow to work at all."),
    S(1, 1, "Mechanism of injury", "Why does the mechanism of injury matter during scene size-up?",
      ["It hints at hidden injuries", "It sets the patient's treatment", "It replaces a physical exam", "It tells you who was at fault"],
      "A high-energy mechanism such as a rollover or a long fall warns you to look for injuries you cannot yet see. It guides your suspicion and your resource request but never replaces assessing the patient."),
    S(1, 2, "Number of patients", "At a two-car crash, the driver you reach says his daughter was in the back seat. The back seat is empty. What should you do next?",
      ["Search the area for the child", "Treat the driver and ignore the remark", "Assume a relative already picked her up", "Wait for police to search later"],
      "Finding every patient is part of size-up. A child may have walked away or been thrown from the car. Look nearby and tell incoming units there may be another patient."),
    S(1, 2, "Resource request", "You arrive alone to find a bus on its side with many people injured. What is your first action?",
      ["Call for more resources", "Begin CPR on the first patient", "Splint the closest open fracture", "Help the walking patients leave"],
      "When patients outnumber you, call early for more units, supervisors and incident command support. One responder who stops to treat one patient cannot help the rest."),
    S(1, 3, "SALT triage", "Using SALT, you ask patients who can walk to move to a set area. What is the next global sort step?",
      ["Ask the rest to wave or move a limb", "Tag every walking patient as minimal before moving on", "Begin CPR on anyone who is not breathing", "Open the airway of every child you can reach"],
      "SALT sorts first by walking, then asks those who cannot walk to wave or show purposeful movement. Those who do neither are assessed first, then those who wave, then the walkers. Tags come later in the individual assessment."),
    M(1, 2, "Hazardous materials", "You see a tanker leaking a cloud from its valve. Which actions are correct? Select two.",
      ["Stay uphill and upwind", "Look up the placard in the ERG", "Walk closer to read the label", "Drive through the cloud quickly", "Treat patients in the cloud first"],
      "Distance and position protect you: stay uphill and upwind and identify the product from a safe distance, using the placard number and the Emergency Response Guidebook. Entering the cloud is for trained hazmat teams with the right protection."),
    O(1, 2, "Emergency moves", "A patient is down in a smoke-filled room and must be moved now. Put the steps in order.",
      ["Make sure you can enter safely", "Grab the clothing at the shoulders", "Support the head between your arms", "Drag the patient along the long axis", "Move to a safe area outside"],
      "An emergency move is only for an immediate danger. Confirm your own safety, grip the shoulders of the clothing, cradle the head, pull in a straight line along the body to limit spine movement and get clear of the hazard."),
    X(1, 2, "Scene hazards", "For each situation, choose whether it is safe to approach now or you must wait for another agency.",
      ["A domestic fight still going on", "A conscious fall victim in a park", "A car on fire with fuel leaking", "A patient in a deep silo", "A child who fell at a playground"],
      ["Approach now", "Wait for help"], [1, 0, 1, 1, 0],
      "An active fight needs police, a burning car needs fire, and a silo is a confined space with deadly gases that needs a rescue team. A fall victim in a park or at a playground can be reached safely."),
    S(1, 3, "Antidote auto-injector", "After a chemical release at a plant, your partner has a runny nose, pinpoint pupils and muscle twitching. Which treatment is within EMR scope under the national model?",
      ["Agency antidote auto-injector", "IV fluids started at the scene", "An inhaler borrowed from a worker", "Oral glucose in his cheek"],
      "The national scope model lets EMRs use agency-supplied auto-injector antidotes for chemical and hazardous material exposures. IV fluids, inhalers and oral glucose are not EMR skills. Move away from the release and call hazmat."),
    S(1, 1, "Crowd control", "Bystanders press around a patient and block your work. What is the best response?",
      ["Give a calm, clear request to step back", "Shout at them to leave the area at once", "Ignore them and keep working", "Ask the patient to send them away"],
      "A calm, direct request usually works, and you can give a helpful bystander a task such as watching for the ambulance. If the crowd turns hostile, retreat and call police."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Traffic safety", "You stop your car at a highway crash at night. Where is the safest place to park?",
      ["Behind the crash, angled to shield the scene", "In front of the crash with lights off", "Across all lanes to stop the traffic flow", "On the far shoulder across the median, facing traffic"],
      "Parking behind the crash, angled with your lights on, forms a barrier that protects you and the patients from traffic. Wear a high-visibility vest and set warning devices if you have them."),
    S(2, 2, "Carbon monoxide", "You enter a house where two people have headaches and nausea and a space heater is running. You feel dizzy. What should you do?",
      ["Leave and get everyone outside", "Turn off the heater and stay to treat them inside", "Open one window and treat inside", "Give oxygen to the sickest person first"],
      "Headaches and nausea in several people, with you now affected, point to carbon monoxide. Get yourself and the occupants into fresh air, and call fire to check the building. You cannot help anyone if you are overcome."),
    S(2, 1, "PPE choice", "What PPE do you need to take a pulse on a calm patient with intact skin and no fluids present?",
      ["Gloves", "Gloves and a gown", "Gloves and a respirator", "A full face shield and gown"],
      "Gloves are the basic standard precaution for patient contact. Eye protection, gowns and respirators are added when splashes, heavy contamination or airborne disease are expected."),
    S(2, 2, "Dispatch information", "Dispatch sends you to an unresponsive 2-year-old pulled from a bathtub. What should you plan on the way?",
      ["Airway and breathing support right away", "Splinting both legs before any other care", "A long, slow search of the yard for other patients", "A slow, detailed history from the parents first"],
      "A child pulled from water needs immediate airway and breathing care, since drowning causes arrest through lack of oxygen. Ready your ventilation equipment and plan to start rescue breaths early."),
    M(2, 2, "Resource request", "Which findings should prompt a request for advanced life support? Select two.",
      ["Chest pain with cold, clammy skin", "A seizure that will not stop", "A sprained ankle", "A small cut on the hand", "A nosebleed that has stopped"],
      "A patient who may be having a heart attack with poor perfusion, or a seizure that keeps going, may need medications and monitoring beyond the EMR level. The minor injuries can wait for routine transport."),
    S(2, 3, "SALT triage", "In the SALT individual assessment, a patient who is breathing has a pulse, follows commands and has bleeding controlled, but you judge he will likely not survive with the resources at hand. Which category fits?",
      ["Expectant", "Immediate", "Delayed", "Minimal"],
      "SALT places a patient who meets the immediate criteria but is unlikely to survive given current resources in the expectant category. That patient is rechecked as resources arrive."),
    S(2, 2, "Lifesaving interventions", "Which lifesaving step does SALT allow during the individual assessment before you move on?",
      ["Controlling major bleeding", "Splinting a closed fracture", "Taking a full set of vitals", "Bandaging minor scrapes and small cuts"],
      "SALT lets you do quick lifesaving steps such as controlling major bleeding, opening the airway and, for a child, two rescue breaths, then move on. Splints, full vitals and dressings come after triage."),
    S(2, 2, "Violent scene", "While you assess a patient, a family member grows angry and picks up a kitchen knife. What should you do?",
      ["Leave with your partner and call police", "Take the knife away", "Keep treating and ignore him", "Ask him to put the knife away in a kitchen drawer"],
      "When a scene becomes unsafe, your first duty is your own safety. Back away, leave by a route you know, and call police. Do not try to disarm anyone."),
    O(2, 2, "Scene size-up", "Put the parts of a scene size-up in order as you arrive.",
      ["Check the scene for hazards", "Put on the PPE you need", "Note the mechanism or nature of illness", "Count the patients", "Request more resources if needed"],
      "You look for danger before you get out, choose PPE before contact, work out what happened, find every patient and then call for the help the scene needs."),
    X(2, 2, "Respiratory protection", "For each patient, choose the protection you need beyond gloves.",
      ["Suspected measles with a rash", "Cough with suspected tuberculosis", "A clean cut on the arm", "A vomiting patient", "A quiet fainting episode"],
      ["Respirator", "Eye protection", "Gloves only"], [0, 0, 2, 1, 2],
      "Measles and tuberculosis spread through the air and need a fit-tested respirator. Vomit can splash into the eyes, so add eye protection. A clean cut or a faint with no fluids needs gloves only."),
    S(2, 2, "Water rescue", "A swimmer is struggling 20 feet from a dock. You are not trained in water rescue. What should you do?",
      ["Throw a floating object and call for help", "Swim out to tow her back to the dock", "Wade out", "Wait on shore without acting"],
      "Reach or throw, do not go: an untrained rescuer who swims out often drowns too. A ring, cooler or rope gives the swimmer something to hold while trained rescuers come."),
    S(2, 2, "Downed motorcycle", "A motorcycle rider lies in the road on a blind curve and traffic is still moving. What should happen first?",
      ["Protect the scene from traffic", "Remove his helmet right away", "Drag him onto the shoulder", "Start a head-to-toe exam"],
      "Approaching cars are the main hazard. Use your vehicle, lights and warning devices or have police stop traffic before you kneel in the road. Move him only if he is in immediate danger."),
    S(2, 3, "Antidote auto-injector", "Who may receive an agency-supplied antidote auto-injector from an EMR at a chemical exposure under the national model?",
      ["Exposed people, per protocol", "Only the EMR's own partner on the call", "No one, unless an EMT is standing beside the EMR", "Only patients older than 65"],
      "The scope model lists auto-injector antidotes for chemical and hazardous material exposures at every level from EMR up, given to exposed people under medical direction and protocol."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "Scene safety", "You arrive at a crash where a pickup lies on its side and rocks slightly. What should you do before reaching the driver?",
      ["Have it stabilized first", "Climb onto it to open a door", "Push it back onto its wheels", "Break the windshield to reach in"],
      "An unstable vehicle can roll onto you or the patient. Wait for fire or rescue to crib and stabilize it before you enter or reach inside."),
    S(0, 1, "Hand hygiene", "When should you clean your hands during a call?",
      ["Before and after patient contact", "Only when they look dirty", "After gloves come off", "Only if you touched blood or vomit directly"],
      "Clean your hands before you touch a patient and after contact, including after taking off gloves. Gloves can have tiny holes, and hands pick up germs from the gloves as they come off."),
    S(0, 2, "Removing gloves", "Why do you peel the first glove off inside out and hold it in the other gloved hand?",
      ["To keep the outside from touching skin", "To save the gloves for reuse", "To make the used gloves easier to recycle", "To check the gloves for tears after use"],
      "Turning the gloves inside out traps the contaminated outer surface inside. Your bare skin then touches only the clean inner side of the second glove."),
    S(0, 2, "Exposure", "You have flushed a patient's blood out of your eye. What must you do next?",
      ["Report the exposure right away", "Wait to see if you feel sick", "Report it at the next shift meeting", "Keep it private to avoid testing"],
      "Report a blood exposure to your supervisor at once so you can be evaluated quickly. Some preventive treatments work best when started within hours."),
    S(0, 2, "Hazardous materials", "Which marking on a truck tells you it carries hazardous material you can look up?",
      ["A diamond placard with a four-digit number", "A company logo painted on the driver door", "A license plate from another state or country", "A speed limiter sticker"],
      "Diamond placards show the hazard class, and the four-digit identification number can be looked up in the Emergency Response Guidebook from a safe distance."),
    S(0, 3, "SALT triage", "During the SALT global sort, a man lies still and does not respond to your commands. What happens next?",
      ["He is assessed first", "He is tagged dead at once", "He is moved to the walkers", "He is skipped until the end"],
      "Patients who neither walk nor wave are assessed first because they are most likely to need lifesaving care. A tag is given only after the individual assessment."),
    S(0, 2, "Pediatric triage", "In SALT, a child is not breathing after you open the airway. What should you do?",
      ["Give two rescue breaths", "Tag the child dead at once", "Start full CPR and stay", "Move on without any action"],
      "SALT allows two rescue breaths for a child who is not breathing after the airway is opened, since a child's arrest often starts with breathing. If breathing does not start, the child is tagged dead and you move on."),
    M(0, 2, "Fire safety", "You reach a house fire before fire crews. Which actions are right? Select two.",
      ["Stay outside the building", "Report people still inside", "Enter to search bedrooms", "Open doors to let smoke out", "Climb a ladder to a window"],
      "Without turnout gear and breathing apparatus, stay out, keep bystanders back and give arriving crews what you know about people inside. Opening doors can feed the fire."),
    S(0, 1, "Planning", "Why is it useful to know the patient's age and complaint from dispatch?",
      ["It lets you prepare equipment", "It confirms the diagnosis", "It allows you to skip the scene size-up", "It tells you the patient's full medical history"],
      "Dispatch details help you plan, such as readying a pediatric mask or the AED. They can be wrong, so you still size up the scene and assess the patient."),
    S(0, 2, "Confined space", "A worker has collapsed at the bottom of a sewer access hole. What should you do?",
      ["Stay out and call a rescue team", "Climb down quickly to check his pulse", "Lower a rope and pull him up", "Send a coworker down a ladder to help"],
      "Confined spaces may lack oxygen or hold toxic gas, which is why the worker collapsed. Would-be rescuers often die there. Only trained teams with air supplies should enter."),
    X(0, 2, "Resource request", "For each situation, choose the resource to request.",
      ["A car door jammed around a trapped driver", "A crowd threatening the crew", "A gas smell in a building", "A long ground trip for a critical patient"],
      ["Fire or rescue", "Law enforcement", "Air medical"], [0, 1, 0, 2],
      "Extrication and gas leaks need fire or rescue, threats need law enforcement, and air medical can shorten a long trip for a critical patient where local protocol allows."),
    S(0, 2, "Weapons", "While moving a patient you find a handgun in his waistband. What should you do?",
      ["Stop and get law enforcement to secure it", "Unload it yourself", "Hide it in your kit", "Leave it in place and keep moving him to the car"],
      "Weapons are a scene hazard. Do not handle the gun; tell police and let them secure it before care continues."),
    S(0, 2, "Hazardous materials", "Two people who were in a spill have chemical powder on their clothes. What should happen before you treat them?",
      ["They are decontaminated first", "You treat them in your vehicle", "You brush the powder off with your hands", "They wait until the hospital"],
      "Contaminated patients can expose you and everyone else. Hazmat teams decontaminate them first, often by removing clothing and rinsing, unless you have the training and gear to help."),
    O(0, 2, "Removing PPE", "Following the CDC's first example sequence, put the steps for taking off PPE in order.",
      ["Remove the gloves", "Remove the goggles or face shield", "Remove the gown", "Remove the mask or respirator", "Clean your hands"],
      "In the CDC's first example sequence the most contaminated items come off first: gloves, then eye protection by the clean band, then the gown, then the mask or respirator by the straps, and hands are cleaned at the end."),
]
